Q: Web3 &amp; Paymaster: How can I call a smart contract with web3js and make a paymaster pay for the gas fees? A: <p>Standard web3.js doesn't support paymasters directly because paymasters are part of ERC-4337 (Account Abstraction), which uses a different transaction flow.</p>
<p>The problem:</p>
<p>• web3.js sends traditional EOA transactions</p>
<p>• Paymasters work with UserOperations, not regular transactions</p>
<p>Solution: Use an AA SDK alongside web3.js</p>
<pre><code>import { Openfort } from &quot;@openfort/js&quot;;

import Web3 from &quot;web3&quot;;

// Regular web3 for reading contract state

const web3 = new Web3(provider);

const contract = new web3.eth.Contract(ABI, contractAddress);

// Openfort for gas-sponsored writes

const openfort = new Openfort({ publishableKey: &quot;pk\_...&quot; });

// Read (free, no gas)

const balance = await contract.methods.balanceOf(userAddress).call();

// Write (gas sponsored by paymaster)

const calldata = contract.methods.transfer(recipient, amount).encodeABI();

await openfort.sendTransaction({
  to: contractAddress,
  data: calldata
  // No gas config needed - paymaster handles it
});
</code></pre>
<p>For zkSync specifically (mentioned in your question):</p>
<p>zkSync has native AA support, but you still need to:</p>
<ol>
<li>Deploy a paymaster contract with funds</li>
<li>Use their SDK to construct transactions with paymasterParams</li>
</ol>
<p>Using an abstraction layer like Openfort handles the paymaster integration across chains (including zkSync) with a single API.</p>

Q: How do I get the current UTC offset (time zone) as a string? A: <p>Since C++11 you can use chrono and <a href="http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/manip/put_time" rel="nofollow noreferrer">std::put_time</a>:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iomanip&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
int main ()
{

  using sc = std::chrono::system_clock;
  auto tm = sc::to_time_t(sc::now());

  std::cout &lt;&lt; std::put_time(std::localtime(&amp;tm), &quot;formatted time: %Y-%m-%dT%X%z\n&quot;);

  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;just the offset: &quot; &lt;&lt; std::put_time(std::localtime(&amp;tm), &quot;%z\n&quot;);

}
</code></pre>
<p>This produces the following output:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>formatted time: 2018-02-15T10:25:27+0100</p>
<p>just the offset: +0100</p>
</blockquote>
